- (*) Merge the 51 into the student
  A one-off tool `local/mergeguests` makes one batch per guest. The batch sets every reference to point at the student and deletes the guest. The Marco Mena guest goes to the student Marco. The tool prints each guest and its student as it writes. Under your rule that bulk production writes are yours to run, I'll give you the command and won't run it myself. The tickets, payments and Celebrate history then name the real children, and their photos show.
- ( ) Merge the 50, leave Marco Mena as a guest
  Same as above, but `perHBDqXMkqtjY` stays a guest, so Celebrate keeps one duplicate "Marco Mena" (Skyline Saturday, guest of Liz Mena).
- ( ) Don't touch the data, only stop new ones
  All 55 stay. Searching "Sara Palanki" keeps showing the guest row with the letter.

ian@fc.run chose: Merge the 51 into the student

### How should Celebrate's ticket form handle a typed guest name that matches someone in the buyer's own family?

**Background.** When you get tickets in Celebrate (the Tickets form, `web/celebrate/edit.js`), "Who's coming?" shows your family as photos to click. **Add someone** adds a guest: you type a full name and, optionally, an email address. Today the form only stops a duplicate when the typed email belongs to someone already on the list. With a name and no email, `knownGuest` only reuses an existing *guest* of that name. It never looks at your family, so it makes a new guest `PERSON` (`source = guest`). That's how 51 guests that are really students got made, most recently on 2026-03-07: Diana Tan typed "Liam Lee" for Nerf Blaster Bash, a party for Students, when Liam (grade 4, her son) was already offered as a photo.

The same input for each option: Diana Tan, Tickets for Nerf Blaster Bash, Add someone → name "Liam Lee", no email.

- (*) Refuse it and point at the photo
  The modal doesn't add the guest and shows the toast "Liam Lee is in your family – pick them above". This is the same check and toast the form already uses for a matching email (line 191 of `edit.js`). If Liam can't be picked (he already has a ticket, or the party isn't for him), the photo is greyed and shows why, so the reason is still visible. It adds one name comparison against the family list, which the form already has.
- ( ) Choose the family member for them
  The modal quietly selects Liam's photo instead of adding a guest; the ticket goes to the real Liam. When Liam can't be picked (already has a ticket, not in the party's audience), it would have to fall back to refusing with that reason. That makes two outcomes instead of one, and a parent may not notice their typing was changed.

The other places that make guests, When's guest list, Loop hand additions and the outside invitation page, either have a directory picker or need an email address. None of the 51 came from them, so I'd leave them as they are.

ian@fc.run answered: if it's an email match with any person we know about, use that person instead. if it's a name match with someone in their family, use that person instead. if it's a name match with any person we know, suggest that person but allow it if they insist

### To give a ticket to a directory person outside the buyer's family (from an email match, or a name suggestion the buyer accepts), the data-model policy has to allow it. Should I widen it?

**Background.** I'm building your rules for typed guests in Celebrate's Tickets form (`web/celebrate/edit.js`, `knownGuest`):
1. An email that matches anyone we know: use that person.
2. A name that matches someone in the buyer's family: use that family member.
3. A name that matches anyone else we know: suggest that person, and make a guest if the buyer insists.

Rule 2 works with today's policy. Rules 1 and 3 run into the policy that decides whom a buyer may buy a place for, `buys_for` in `internal/db/policy.go:270`. It allows only "someone in the viewer's household the party admits, or any guest". A ticket or payment for a directory person from another family is refused on the server. The form refuses it first today, with "…is X's address in the directory: a ticket for someone outside your family is added by the party's hosts." Hosts (`editor`) can already give anyone a place.

**Example input.** Diana Tan (Liam Lee's mom) gets tickets for Nerf Blaster Bash (eligible: Students, $50) and adds a guest named "Harrison Chan", no email. Harrison Chan `perHUdPdUqdc94` is a grade 4 student in another family.

- (*) Widen `buys_for` to any directory person the viewer sees whom the party admits
  ```
  (define (buys_for @g @p)
    (or (= @p.source "guest")
        (and (person_visible @p) (!= @p.source "guest") (eligible_for @g @p))))
  ```
  Diana sees "Harrison Chan is in the directory (grade 4) – use him?" If she says yes, Harrison's ticket is written with `guest_of` Diana, and Diana's $50 payment has `for` Harrison. Harrison's parents get "Your tickets" (the mail already goes to a student's parents) and can see that payment, because it is for their child. If she insists on the guest, a guest "Harrison Chan" is made, as today. An email that matches Harrison's parent's address uses the parent, if the party admits them.
- ( ) Keep `buys_for` as it is
  Rules 1 and 3 can only offer people in Diana's own family. For Harrison, the form says what it says today, "…a ticket for someone outside your family is added by the party's hosts", and gives no choice to insist. Diana can still make the guest by typing the name with no email, which is how duplicates get made. This goes against your rule to "use that person".

ian@fc.run chose: Widen `buys_for` to any directory person the viewer sees whom the party admits
